>
  <Fragment slot="lede">A year of building a chat where every non-text element is an <mark class="hl">LLM tool call</mark>. Why we started by asking the model for one big JSON document, why that ceiling turned out to be structural rather than fixable, and what changed when we pivoted to one tool call per component.</Fragment>

  <p><strong>Strive AI</strong> is our generative UI chat engine, the gateway to the agentic marketing OS we’ve built at <a href="https://strivelabs.ai/" rel="noopener">Strivelabs</a>. I’ve been on the frontend of it since day zero.</p>

  <blockquote>A chat that answers in UI, not just text.</blockquote>

  <p>It’s been a year now. This post is a reflection on the full ride: how it started, why we built it from the ground up, the challenges we faced, the lessons we learned, where we stand today, and what’s next.</p>

  <h2>The one-liner</h2>
  <p>The user brings the intent. The agent turns it into an interface.</p>
  <p>Every non-text element in a response is an individual <strong>LLM tool call</strong>. The tool’s Zod schema <em>is</em> the React component’s prop type. Arguments stream over SSE and render progressively.</p>

  <OneTurnOneStream />

  <h2>The fundamental pieces</h2>
  <p>Under the hood, these are the pieces that make the engine tick. Everything else in chat sits on top of them.</p>

  <h3>Blocks: the unit of a reply</h3>
  <p>A block is one renderable unit in an assistant response. It has a <strong>type</strong>, a <strong>version</strong>, a <strong>content</strong> payload, and optionally a list of <strong>behaviours</strong>.</p>

  <BlockAnatomy />

  <h3>Renderers: dumb on purpose</h3>
  <p>Each block type has a React renderer that takes the block’s content as its props and draws it. Renderers know nothing about the model, streaming, or the conversation. Hand one a block and it renders. That strictness is what makes replaying history trivial: the same renderer draws a live block and a two-week-old one.</p>

  <RendererMapping />

  <h3>The streaming parser: bytes are not blocks</h3>
  <p>The model’s output arrives as a byte stream. Something has to sit in between and decide, continuously, whether a complete block has arrived yet. The parser watches the buffer, detects block boundaries before the surrounding JSON is valid, emits text deltas character by character, and signals when a block is complete enough to hand to a renderer.</p>

  <StreamingParser />

  <h3>The event vocabulary: one stream, one handler</h3>
  <p>Everything travels over a single SSE stream with a small set of event types: streaming content, streaming reasoning, tool calls, tool-argument streaming (start and content), conversation metadata, and citations. One handler in the client switches on these.</p>
  <blockquote>The entire chat is that handler plus state.</blockquote>

  <h3>Behaviours and slots: messages that stay alive</h3>
  <p>A block can carry a <strong>behaviour</strong>: an attached action, triggered either by a click or automatically on render. A click behaviour usually starts a new chat turn. An automatic one mounts a live agent view inside the message, a component that fetches its own data, polls, holds editable state, and writes back. We call those <strong>slots</strong>. They are the reason a chat message can be a task queue that is still updating while you scroll past it.</p>

  <BehaviourSlot />

  <h3>The session context: a stream that outlives the view</h3>
  <p>Underneath all of it sits a session that outlives the view: messages, parser, streaming indices, pending interactions and abort controller, keyed per conversation. Navigate away mid-response and the stream keeps running. Come back and it’s complete.</p>

  <h2>A little bit of backstory</h2>
  <p>We built this system from the ground up. That was a conscious, tough call, and one we kept revisiting along the way. Starting small with our own layer, on top of what we already had, was the cheapest option that kept every door open.</p>
  <p>It all started in <strong>September 2025</strong>. Generative UI was still new, with few proven implementations at scale, but chat was quickly becoming the interface for agents.</p>
  <blockquote>Thanks to our co-founders for spotting the bet early, backing us to go all in, and never letting us stop poking at what’s next.</blockquote>
  <p>Before any of it existed, it looked like this.</p>

